# Can markets fix their own information problems?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Warranties, brands, reviews, inspections: how far do they go? When do they fail?"
next: {"title":"Markets fail — so the state should step in?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/52-markets-fail-so-the-state-should-step-in.cast"}

## What a good name is worth
sources:
    - id: resnick2006
      title: 'The value of reputation on eBay: A controlled experiment'
      authors: Resnick, P., Zeckhauser, R., Swanson, J. & Lockwood, K.
      year: 2006
      finding: An established, high-reputation eBay dealer sold matched lots of vintage postcards under his own identity and under new identities; buyers' willingness to pay was 8.1% higher for the established identity.
      url: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/experimental-economics/article/abs/value-of-reputation-on-ebay-a-controlled-experiment/610EA11BA19583907061AFB677BE4338

    icon seller size 150 set twemoji x 120 y 470 or ["convenience store","store"] icon_look picture of shop hidden true
    text seller_lbl "knows quality" #2f6b8f x 120 y 370 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon buyer size 150 set twemoji x 880 y 470 or ["man","standing person"] icon_look picture of person hidden true
    text buyer_q "?" #b5482e x 880 y 610 font_size 80 hidden true
    icon parcel size 110 set twemoji x 500 y 530 or ["box"] icon_look picture of package hidden true
    text stars "★★★★★" #e0a21b x 500 y 445 font_size 44 hidden true
    text rec_lbl "seller's record" x 500 y 395 font_size 30 hidden true
    scale guessline cites ["resnick2006"] x 150 y 190 width 620 font_size 30 label "same postcards: extra price for the old name" unit % min 0 max 30 value 8.1 ticks 6 hidden true
    box plank_w "Warranty\nseller's money" x 355 y 505 width 280 height 150 icon ["shield"] font_size 34 hidden true
    box plank_b "Brand\nseller's future" x 645 y 505 width 280 height 150 icon ["label","tag"] font_size 34 hidden true
    box plank_r "Reviews\nother buyers" x 355 y 345 width 280 height 150 icon ["star"] font_size 34 hidden true
    box plank_i "Inspection\na third party" x 645 y 345 width 280 height 150 icon ["magnifying glass","magnifier"] font_size 34 hidden true
    group planks members ["plank_w","plank_b","plank_r","plank_i"] hidden true
    text moral "lying must cost the liar" #b5482e x 500 y 630 font_size 36 hidden true
    text big81 "+8.1%" #b5482e cites ["resnick2006"] x 890 y 190 font_size 60 hidden true

    card title "What a good name is worth"

When the seller knows some (@draw seller_lbl@)thing you don't, can the market itself (@draw buyer_q@) make it safe to buy?
    draw seller buyer parallel true

Picture sending money to a stranger, for a thing you've only seen in a photo.
    draw parcel

    pause 0.3

B: Which is just eBay. People do it millions of times a day, with nothin (@draw stars@)g to go on but a user name and a row of stars.

Those stars are the seller's record: every past buyer's verdict, added up beside the name.
    draw rec_lbl

A team of economists once tested what that record is worth. A dealer with a long, spotless record sold ma (@draw guessline@)tched lots of vintage postcards, some under his own name, some under brand-new names.

B: Same postcards, same dealer, only the name differs.
    highlight guessline

    ask "How much more did the old name fetch? Click the line at your guess." on guessline store g default "15" voice b
        right: Close: you said {g}. It was 8.1%.
        wrong: You said {g}. It was 8.1%.

About e (@draw big81@)ight percent more. That's the price of a good name, on every sale.

    pause 0.5

B: Eight percent? That's hardly a fortune.
    highlight guessline effect underline

On one sale, no. But it's what makes honesty pay: a seller who cheats once risks that extra on every sale to come.
    highlight stars

    erase guessline big81

Underneath is one problem. The seller knows the (@highlight seller_lbl buyer_q@) quality, the buyer doesn't, and left alone that gap can shrink a market until only the junk is worth selling.
    erase parcel stars rec_lbl

Markets have grown four kinds of bridge across it. A w (@draw plank_w@)arranty, where the seller bets his own money.

A brand, where he bets his future sales.
    draw plank_b

B: Reviews, where other buyers remember for you.
    draw plank_r

B: And an inspection, where someone else checks before you pay.
    draw plank_i

When they work, all four work the same way: they make a lie cost the liar more than it earns.
    draw moral

The question is when that's true.
    highlight planks effect box

## Why a promise is believed
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 105
    'y':
      - 0
      - 2000
    box:
      x: 20
      'y': 150
      w: 680
      h: 500
vars:
    prem:
      value: 800
      min: 0
      max: 2000
    cut:
      expr: min(100, prem/20)
    ps:
      expr: prem - 200
    pd:
      expr: prem - 1200
sources:
    - id: spence1973
      title: Job market signaling
      authors: Spence, M.
      year: 1973
      finding: A costly action can credibly reveal hidden quality when it is cheaper for the high-quality type than for the low-quality type.
    - id: grossman1981
      title: The informational role of warranties and private disclosure about product quality
      authors: Grossman, S. J.
      year: 1981
      finding: Warranties can reveal product quality, because offering one is cheaper for a seller of a reliable product.
    - id: kleinleffler1981
      title: The role of market forces in assuring contractual performance
      authors: Klein, B. & Leffler, K. B.
      year: 1981
      finding: A firm keeps its quality promises when the stream of future price premiums it would lose by cheating exceeds the one-time gain from cheating.

    axes ax x_label "breakdown chance (%)" y_label "warranty cost to the yard ($)" cross corner ticks.x [20,40,60] ticks.y [500,1000,1500,2000] hidden true
    icon boat size 90 set twemoji x 860 y 600 or ["speedboat","boat"] icon_look picture of "motor boat" hidden true
    text boat_lbl "used engines" x 860 y 535 font_size 28 hidden true
    text repair "repair: $2,000" x 860 y 480 font_size 30 hidden true
    curve costline #2f6b8f expr 20*x x_from 0 x_to 100 style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    label l_cost "chance × $2,000" above-left #2f6b8f attach_to costline font_size 26 hidden true
    dot sound #4a8a3a at.on costline at.x 10 hidden true
    label l_sound "sound: $200" right #4a8a3a attach_to sound font_size 26 hidden true
    dot dud #b5482e at.on costline at.x 60 hidden true
    label l_dud "dud: $1,200" right #b5482e attach_to dud font_size 26 hidden true
    curve premline dashed thick #8a5fa8 expr prem x_from 0 x_to 100 hidden true
    label l_prem "premium ${prem:0,}" right #8a5fa8 attach_to premline font_size 26 hidden true
    dot cutpt #8a5fa8 at.on costline at.x 40 bind {"at.x":"cut"} hidden true
    region pays x_from 0 x_to.ref cutpt between ["costline","premline"] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.45 hidden true
    curve cutline dashed #8f887c x_expr cut y_expr t t_from 0 t_to 1800 style.stroke_width 2 hidden true
    label l_cut "cut-off {cut:0}%" above #8f887c attach_to cutline font_size 26 hidden true
    text r_head "gain per warranty ($)" x 860 y 400 font_size 28 hidden true
    text r_s "sound: {ps:0,}" #4a8a3a x 860 y 350 font_size 32 hidden true
    text r_d "dud: {pd:0,}" #b5482e x 860 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text cue_signal "a signal:\ncheap only for the honest" #3d3833 cites ["spence1973","grossman1981"] x 850 y 215 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cue_brand "brand = a warranty\non all future sales" #8a5fa8 cites ["kleinleffler1981"] x 850 y 215 font_size 28 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mcard center work [{"tex":"\\text{warranty costs the seller } p \\cdot R"},{"tex":"\\text{offer it if premium} > p \\cdot R"},{"tex":"p_{sound} R < \\text{premium} < p_{dud} R"},{"tex":"200 < 800 < 1200"},{"tex":"\\text{brand: one-time gain} < \\text{future premiums}"}] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Why a promise is believed"

B: So when does a promise make lying expensive? Take Harbourtown's boatyard, selling second-hand outboard engines.
    draw ax

B: The numbers are illustrative.
    draw boat boat_lbl

And I, the buyer, can't tell a sound engine from a dud by looking.
    highlight boat

B: No, but the yard can. Say a sound engine has a one-in-ten chance of breaking down in its first year, a dud six in ten, and a re (@draw repair@)pair costs two thousand dollars.

B: Across, the chance of a breakdown; up, what a one-year warranty costs the yard on average.
    draw costline l_cost

B: For the sound engine, two hundred dollars.
    draw sound l_sound

And for the dud, twelve hundred. Same promise, six times the cost.
    draw dud l_dud

    pause 0.3

B: Now say buyers pay eight hundred dollars extra for an engine that comes with a warranty.
    draw premline l_prem

Then the yard puts warranties on the sound engines and makes six hundred on each,
    draw r_head r_s

and keeps them off the duds, where it would lose four hundred.
    draw r_d

B: That's why buyers can believe it. The promise (@draw cue_signal@) is cheap only for the honest seller. Economists call that a signal.

And where the premium line crosses the cost line, at forty percent, is the cut-off: an engine any shakier than that isn't worth the promise.
    draw cutpt cutline l_cut pays

Your turn: drag the premium up past twelve hundred dollars and watch the dud's number.
    explore params ["prem"]

    animate prem 800 duration 0.5

B: Now the dud seller offers the warranty too.
    animate prem 1500 duration 2.5

B: It no longer tells buyers anything, so they won't keep paying extra for it.
    highlight r_d dud

    animate prem 800 duration 1.5

    erase cue_signal

A brand is the same idea stretched over time: a name is a warranty on every future sale, and one round of cheating puts all of it at risk.
    draw cue_brand

    pause 0.3

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own store m default "Skip it"

@math

    erase mathq

    hide boat boat_lbl repair r_head r_s r_d cue_brand

    fade ax costline l_cost sound l_sound dud l_dud premline l_prem cutpt pays cutline l_cut to 0.15

Call the repair cost R and the chance of a breakdown p. A warranty costs the seller p times R.
    draw mcard_box mcard_line_1

It pays to offer one when the premium is bigger than that.
    draw mcard_line_2

So it sorts the engines only if the premium falls between the sound one's cost and the dud's,
    draw mcard_line_3

here two hundred, eight hundred, twelve hundred.
    draw mcard_line_4

A brand obeys the same rule over time: it stays honest while one sale's gain from cheating is smaller than all the future premiums it would lose.
    draw mcard_line_5

    erase mcard

@test

    erase mathq

    show boat boat_lbl repair r_head r_s r_d cue_brand

    fade ax costline l_cost sound l_sound dud l_dud premline l_prem cutpt pays cutline l_cut to 1

    pause 0.3

## Where the fixes crack
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: 'You''d make a fine surveyor: hard to fool.'
    good: Mostly right. Ask who finds out, and who pays the checker.
    poor: 'Ask two things: will the truth come out, and does the liar pay for it?'
    none: 'Stars, warranties, inspectors: each works only where a lie costs the liar.'
sources:
    - id: noskotadelis2015
      title: 'The limits of reputation in platform markets: An empirical analysis and field experiment'
      authors: Nosko, C. & Tadelis, S.
      year: 2015
      finding: In their eBay data, sellers' percent-positive feedback has a mean of 99.3% and a median of 100%; unhappy buyers often leave no feedback and leave the platform.
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w20830
    - id: lucazervas2016
      title: 'Fake it till you make it: Reputation, competition, and Yelp review fraud'
      authors: Luca, M. & Zervas, G.
      year: 2016
      finding: About 16% of Yelp reviews of Boston restaurants were filtered by Yelp as suspicious; restaurants with weak or declining reputations were more likely to commit review fraud.
    - id: darbykarni1973
      title: Free competition and the optimal amount of fraud
      authors: Darby, M. R. & Karni, E.
      year: 1973
      finding: 'Introduces credence qualities: qualities a buyer cannot judge even after use, which leave room for fraud that reputation cannot fully discipline.'
    - id: fcic2011
      title: The Financial Crisis Inquiry Report
      authors: Financial Crisis Inquiry Commission
      year: 2011
      finding: In 2006 Moody's rated about 30 mortgage-related securities triple-A every working day; 83% of the mortgage securities rated triple-A that year were ultimately downgraded. The ratings were paid for by the firms that issued the securities.
      url: https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-FCIC/pdf/GPO-FCIC.pdf
    - id: lloydsreg
      title: Classification symbols 1764–1994
      authors: Lloyd's Register Foundation Heritage & Education Centre
      year: 2020
      finding: Lloyd's Register, formed in 1760 by customers of Lloyd's coffee house, classed ships from 1764; its hull-letter and equipment-number grades gave the expression 'A1'.
      url: https://heritage.lrfoundation.org.uk/classification-symbols-1764-1994

    text h_hold "Holds when" #4a8a3a x 270 y 645 font_size 38 hidden true
    text h_crack "Cracks when" #b5482e x 730 y 645 font_size 38 hidden true
    path rule_h #8f887c points [[70,612],[930,612]] hidden true
    path rule_v #8f887c points [[500,665],[500,370]] hidden true
    text r1h "ratings stay honest" x 270 y 565 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r1c "ratings inflate or are faked" x 730 y 565 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r2h "buyers find out soon" x 270 y 485 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r2c "you never find out" x 730 y 490 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r2tag "(credence goods)" #8f887c cites ["darbykarni1973"] x 730 y 452 font_size 26 hidden true
    text r3h "checker works for the buyer" x 270 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text r3c "checker paid by seller" x 730 y 400 font_size 34 hidden true
    text stat_ebay "eBay: average seller 99.3% positive" #2f6b8f cites ["noskotadelis2015"] x 500 y 280 font_size 34 hidden true
    text stat_yelp "Boston, Yelp: ~1 review in 6 filtered as suspect" #2f6b8f cites ["lucazervas2016"] x 500 y 280 font_size 34 hidden true
    text stat_moody "Moody's, 2006: ~30 triple-A ratings a working day" #2f6b8f cites ["fcic2011"] x 500 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stat_moody2 "83% later downgraded" #b5482e cites ["fcic2011"] x 500 y 245 font_size 40 hidden true
    text stat_lloyd "Lloyd's Register, 1700s: ships graded \"A1\"" #4a8a3a cites ["lloydsreg"] x 500 y 280 font_size 34 hidden true
    text conf1 "easy cases: mostly agreed" #4a8a3a x 270 y 305 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Where the fixes crack"

A promise persuades only when it's cheap for the honest seller. So where does that crack in real markets?
    draw h_hold h_crack rule_h rule_v

B: In the stars themselves.
    draw r1c

B: In one big eBay study, the average seller was over ninety-nine percent positive.
    draw stat_ebay

And when nearly everyone is at ninety-nine, the number stops sorting anyone. Unhappy buyers often just leave without a word.
    highlight stat_ebay

    erase stat_ebay

B: Then there's faking. Among Boston restaurants, Yelp's own filter set aside about one review in six as suspect.
    draw stat_yelp

    erase stat_yelp

Second crack: some things (@draw r2c@) you never learn, even after you've bought them. Did the mechanic really need to replace that part?

B: Economists call those credence goods. A review can't report what the reviewer never found out.
    draw r2tag

Third crack: who  (@draw r3c@)pays the checker. In 2006 Moody's gave its top rating, tripl (@draw stat_moody@)e-A, to about thirty mortgage securities every working day, paid by the banks that issued them.

B: And eighty-three percent of that year's triple-A mortgage securities were later downgraded.
    draw stat_moody2

    pause 0.5

    erase stat_moody stat_moody2

Compare an older rating. In the 1700s, Lloyd's Register graded ships for the underwriters who insured them, and its top grade gave us the phrase A1.
    draw stat_lloyd

    erase stat_lloyd

B: So the private fixes are stron (@draw r1h@)g where ratings stay honest, buyers (@draw r2h@) find out soon, and the checker ans (@draw r3h@)wers to the buyer.

B: They're weakest where the truth stays hidden longest.
    highlight r1c r2c r2tag r3c

How sure are we? That they work in the e (@draw conf1@)asy cases is mostly agreed.

How far they reach in the hard ones is contested.
    text conf2 "hard cases: contested" #b5482e x 730 y 305 font_size 32

    quiz "Which purchase can online reviews help you with least?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: The pill: no reviewer can tell whether it cut their risk, so the stars measure nothing.
        wrong: Could the person writing the review ever have found out whether it worked? Here, the pill.
        * A hotel room for next weekend
        * A pair of running shoes
        + A pill that claims to cut your heart risk in 20 years

    hide h_hold h_crack rule_h rule_v r1h r1c r2h r2c r2tag r3h r3c conf1 conf2

    cards sortc check each
        Fix likely holds: Chain hotel on a busy road
        Likely cracks: Cruise-port souvenir stall, Mechanic's advice
        Fix likely holds: Bakery full of regulars

    ask "Will the market's own fix hold, or crack? Sort each seller." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All four right: regulars and a busy chain hold, since they have a future to lose; the port stall never sees you again, and you can't check the mechanic.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try. Regulars and a busy chain hold; the port stall and the mechanic crack.

    erase sortc

    show h_hold h_crack rule_h rule_v r1h r1c r2h r2c r2tag r3h r3c conf1 conf2

    quiz "You are buying a used fishing boat and want its hull surveyed. Whose surveyor is most likely to tell you the truth?"
        right: One you choose and pay: a checker paid by the buyer loses business by going easy, one paid by the seller gains by it.
        wrong: Whose money is lost if the surveyor goes easy on the boat? Here, one you choose and pay.
        * One the seller chooses and pays
        + One you choose and pay
        * One the seller's bank recommends

So can markets fix their own information problems? Often, yes: wherever a lie gets found out and costs the liar.
    highlight h_hold h_crack

Where it never does, someone else must step in, and whether that someone should be the state is the next question.
    highlight h_crack effect underline

## Where next
